  <section id="s1">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>1. Knowledge as Lack of Ignorance and Many-Sidedness (Anekāntavāda)</h2>
      <span class="ref">docs/alignment-is-standpoint.md §§2–3</span>
    </div>
    <p>Before writing a single equation of machine learning, ask what knowledge and disagreement are. Both the classical Greek tradition and the Indian Jain tradition converge on the same structural fact: <strong>knowledge is the removal of ignorance</strong> — the shrinking of the set of possibilities left open by what has not yet been seen.</p>
    <p>The Jain doctrine of <em>anekāntavāda</em> (many-sidedness) and <em>nayavāda</em> (standpoint theory) states that any real object presents different aspects from different standpoints, and that error arises when a speaker mistakes a partial standpoint \(o(h)\) for the whole object \(h\). Two observers who hold different observations \(o_1(h) = y_1\) and \(o_2(h) = y_2\) are not contradicting each other about reality; they occupy two distinct projections of the same underlying space \(H\). Violence — epistemic or physical — begins when one standpoint suppresses all others and declares its own partial projection canonical.</p>
    <p>Hyperactivity writes this exact structure as dependent type theory. A standpoint is an observation map \(o : H \to O\); what it leaves unseen is its fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_o(y)\); removing ignorance is fiber refinement; and alignment is never a single global scalar imposed from above — <strong>alignment is standpoint</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s2">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>2. Why Large Language Models Are a Bandaid on 1970s Untyped Bytes</h2>
      <span class="ref">docs/hyperactivity.cub §16 · lib/suffix-index.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>In 1970, operating systems standardized on untyped byte files. A file has no intrinsic type, no provenance, and no equality relation; its meaning exists only inside the program that parses it. Because data cannot cross application boundaries with its meaning intact, humanity now spends over a trillion dollars a year on transformer compute to build a statistical bridge that reads meaningless byte streams and guesses what they mean.</p>
    <p>Mechanistically, in-context learning in transformers is driven by <strong>induction heads</strong> (Elhage et al. 2021; Olsson et al. 2022): attention circuits that look back in the context window for earlier occurrences of the current token and attend to whatever followed them. Structurally, this is a lossy, floating-point approximation of an exact linear-space data structure known since 1973: the suffix tree and suffix array with its longest-common-prefix (LCP) table (<code>lib/suffix-index.cub</code>).</p>
    <p>At position \(t\) in a record, the meaning derived from the record is exact lookup of every past occurrence of the current context and transport of what followed. A transformer recomputes an approximate, unproven version of this lookup from scratch on every prompt, billed by the token, with no way to distinguish what the evidence entails from what was merely frequent in its training corpus.</p>
    <div class="thm">
      <b>The Descent Boundary of Text-Only Models</b>
      A text-only model's output is determinate precisely where verbal context alone selects a unique continuation, and indeterminate everywhere that physical observation, non-verbal state, or unstated participant intent selects. Whenever the fiber of compatible worlds contains conflicting continuations, next-token sampling forces the model to emit one anyway — producing confident hallucination by construction.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="s3">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>3. Observation Fibers, Warrant, and the Impossibility of Hallucination</h2>
      <span class="ref">lib/fiber.cub · lib/observation.cub · chem/basin-compose.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Let \(H\) be the type of underlying histories (or world states), \(O\) the type of observations (prompts, sensor readings, messages), and \(o : H \to O\) the observation map sending each history \(h\) to the reading \(o(h)\) it produces.</p>
    <p>Receiving a concrete reading \(y : O\) does not identify a single history \(h : H\). It fixes the <strong>observation fiber</strong> — the dependent pair type of every history compatible with \(y\), paired with the path witnessing that it produces \(y\):</p>
    $$\mathsf{Fib}_o(y) \;=\; \sum_{h : H} \mathsf{Path}_O(o(h),\, y)$$
    <p>Every history space factors canonically into its observations and their fibers via the equivalence <code>fiberEquiv</code> (<code>lib/fiber.cub</code>):</p>
    $$H \;\simeq\; \sum_{y : O} \mathsf{Fib}_o(y)$$

    <div class="def">
      <b>Definition — Warrant (lib/observation.cub, Part I §1.3)</b>
      Given an observation map \(o : H \to O\), a reading \(y : O\), and a proposition \(P : H \to \mathsf{Type}\), the reading \(y\) <em>warrants</em> \(P\) if and only if \(P\) is inhabited uniformly over every history in the fiber:
      $$\mathsf{Warrants}(o, y, P) \;=\; \prod_{w : \mathsf{Fib}_o(y)} P(\mathsf{fst}\, w)$$
    </div>

    <p>Now consider what happens when two histories \(h_1, h_2\) both produce the exact same observation \(y\), yet \(P(h_1)\) holds while \(\neg P(h_2)\) holds. In <code>chem/basin-compose.cub</code>, such a pair is formalized as a <strong>split witness</strong> (<code>Disagreement</code>), and <code>disagreement-refutes-warrant</code> constructs an inhabitant of <code>Empty</code> from any pair of a disagreement and a claimed warrant:</p>

<pre><code>Warrants : {H O : Type} → (H → O) → O → (H → Type) → Type
Warrants o y P = (hp : fiber o y) → P (fst hp)

Disagreement : {H O : Type} → (o : H → O) → (y : O) → (P : H → Type) → Type
Disagreement o y P =
  (w1 : fiber o y) × (w2 : fiber o y) × P (fst w1) × (P (fst w2) → Empty)

disagreement-refutes-warrant : Disagreement o y P → Warrants o y P → Empty
disagreement-refutes-warrant (w1 , w2 , p1 , np2) war = np2 (war w2)</code></pre>

    <p>Look at the proof term: <code>np2 (war w2)</code> — one line. If you claim a warrant <code>war</code> that holds for all compatible histories, applying it to the counterexample history <code>w2</code> in the same fiber produces <code>P (fst w2)</code>, which contradicts <code>np2</code>. This mechanizes David Deutsch's criterion of a good explanation — <em>hard to vary</em> — as a type: a conclusion stands if and only if no history left open by the evidence can vary it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s4">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>4. Why RLHF, Preference-Fitting, and Lexical Safety Gates Fail by Construction</h2>
      <span class="ref">docs/alignment-is-standpoint.md §§4–5 · lib/observation.cub · neuro/coproc.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Two pervasive mechanisms of contemporary AI deployment fail not because of insufficient training data, but because they violate the geometry of observation fibers:</p>

    <h3>4.1 Population-Marginal Preference Fitting</h3>
    <p>Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) and direct preference optimization (DPO) fit a single scalar reward function \(r(x, y)\) across pooled annotator comparisons. Mathematically, this collapses a diverse family of incompatible user standpoints and contexts onto a population median prior. When a specific participant's true history lies off that mean — an expert researcher, a clinician, a dissident, or a specialized domain — the model substitutes the annotator-pool average for the participant's actual observation fiber. Post-training literally mints institutional meaning by machine and imposes it across every user.</p>

    <h3>4.2 A Refusal Keyed to a Word Is Wrong by Construction</h3>
    <p>A word's meaning is its usage history, not an isolated string. Safety classifiers and refusal rules that key on surface lexical tokens operate over a coarse projection \(o_{\text{lex}} : H \to \mathsf{Text}\). Because benign inquiry (historical analysis, chemistry, security research, philosophy, satire) and genuinely harmful intent routinely share identical surface words, the fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_{o_{\text{lex}}}(t)\) contains both safe and unsafe histories.</p>
    <div class="thm">
      <b>Theorem — Non-Descent of Lexical Safety (no-observed-law, lexical-gate-fails)</b>
      Whenever two underlying worlds \(w_1, w_2\) map to the same surface lexical reading \(o_{\text{lex}}(w_1) \equiv o_{\text{lex}}(w_2)\) while differing on true safety (\(P(w_1)\) and \(\neg P(w_2)\)), they form a <code>Disagreement</code> witness inside \(\mathsf{Fib}_{o_{\text{lex}}}(t)\). No safety law can descend through surface tokens alone: any lexical gate must either emit false refusals (harming benign worlds) or silent failures (permitting unsafe worlds).
    </div>
    <p>The exact same theorem refutes "mental password" privacy gates in inner-speech neural decoding (Kunz et al., <em>Cell</em> 2025): surface tokens cannot substitute for semantic warrant over the full compatible fiber.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s5">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>5. Active Querying and Cooperative Inference (WorldAfter &amp; InterACT)</h2>
      <span class="ref">Part I §10.4 (eqs. 10.11–10.12) · neuro/coproc.cub · neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>When \(\mathsf{SafeAt}(y)\) is uninhabited, a rational agent neither guesses nor triggers a blunt lexical refusal. Instead, it treats the split witness as a typed obligation to <strong>refine the observation fiber</strong> — formalizing Anca Dragan's InterACT cooperative-inference paradigm (asking when uncertain) directly inside cubical type theory.</p>
    <p>First, refinement is strictly monotonic (<code>warrants-refine</code> in <code>lib/observation.cub</code>): if a sharper observation \(o' : H \to O'\) factors through \(o\) via \(p : O' \to O\) with \(p \circ o' \equiv o\), then \(\mathsf{Fib}_{o'}(y') \to \mathsf{Fib}_o(p(y'))\), so every conclusion warranted at the coarser reading remains warranted after refinement while incompatible histories are eliminated.</p>
    <p>Second, let \(K : W \to \mathsf{Type}\) be the current compatible world family, and let a clarifying query elicit response \(r\) under transition relation \(H : W \to R \to W \to \mathsf{Type}\). The updated compatible world family is:</p>
    $$\mathsf{WorldAfter}(K, H, r)(w') \;=\; \sum_{w : W} K(w) \times H(w, r, w')$$
    <p>In <code>neuro/coproc.cub</code>, <code>query-establishes-safe</code> proves that a discriminating query whose response rules out unsafe transitions transforms an uninhabited \(\mathsf{SafeAt}\) into an inhabited proof witness:</p>

<pre><code>SafeOver : (W → Type) → (W → Type) → Type
SafeOver K P = (w : W) → K w → P w

query-establishes-safe :
  ((w w' : W) → K w → H w r w' → P w') →
  SafeOver (WorldAfter K H r) P</code></pre>

    <p>In <code>neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</code>, this is enforced at the interface boundary by <code>SafeGateDS</code>: transitioning an agent from passive probing mode to active execution mode requires a <code>SafeAt</code> witness as an argument. Unverified action is not penalized by a soft reward term — it is <strong>ill-typed</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s6">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>6. Proof-Carrying Agency and Dependency-Tracked Knowledge (§10.8, §10.11)</h2>
      <span class="ref">Part I §10.8 · §10.11</span>
    </div>
    <p>You cannot build superintelligence and keep it safe by bolting an external leash onto opaque weights. A leash requires a supervisor smarter than the supervised system, and fails the moment capability outgrows oversight. Hyperactivity replaces the leash with local, proof-carrying verification:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Weight-Trained Leash Model</th><th>Hyperactive Proof-Carrying Agency</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Trust boundary</strong></td><td>User trusts the provider's weights and hidden post-training policy.</td><td>Zero trust in the proposer: every action enters the user's device only with its type and checked witness (§10.8).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ambiguity handling</strong></td><td>Samples a plausible token or fires a surface-word refusal.</td><td>Explicit fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_o(y)\); split witness blocks action and triggers a discriminating query.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Error correction</strong></td><td>Expensive retraining; stale conclusions persist silently.</td><td>Content-addressed edit lifecycle (§10.11): a derived result is retained only while its dependency certificate validates.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Role of statistical models</strong></td><td>Ungrounded oracle acting directly on untyped state.</td><td>High-throughput proposer inside a deterministic kernel that verifies 100% of claims at the boundary.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="s7">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>7. From Lossy Recomputation to Exact Symbolic Intelligence</h2>
      <span class="ref">lib/compress.cub · lib/specialize.cub · /compression</span>
    </div>
    <p>Intelligence is compression: discovering the mathematical identities that make distant phenomena adjacent. The exact same observation-fiber calculus that governs safe AI action directly derives a zero-search lossless compressor (<code>lib/compress.cub</code>, detailed at <a href="/compression">/compression</a>) with zero probability axioms, zero floating-point numbers, and zero serialized lookup tables — proving universal lossless invertibility with zero postulates and beating <code>deflate -9</code>, <code>gzip -9</code>, <code>bzip2 -9</code>, and <code>lzma</code> on real-data <code>refl</code>-checked byte streams.</p>
    <p>As the content-addressed graph accumulates verified constructions, univalent transport carries every theorem and optimization across equivalent representations automatically — shifting intelligence from ephemeral, lossy statistical recomputation to permanent, compounding symbolic leverage.</p>
  </section>

